  "text": "The problem states that the 9 members of a baseball team went to an ice cream parlor after their game. Each player had a single scoop cone of chocolate, vanilla, or strawberry ice cream. At least one player chose each flavor, and the number of players who chose chocolate was greater than the number of players who chose vanilla, which was greater than the number of players who chose strawberry.\n\nLet's denote the number of players who chose chocolate as \\( C \\), vanilla as \\( V \\), and strawberry as \\( S \\). The conditions given are:\n1. \\( C + V + S = 9 \\)\n2. \\( C > V \\)\n3. \\( V > S \\)\n\nSince \\( C > V > S \\) and each is at least 1, the possible values for \\( C, V, \\) and \\( S \\) must be integers between 1 and 8 (since \\( C \\) must be at least 2 and at most 8 because \\( C + V + S = 9 \\)).\n\nLet's find the number of valid assignments for each combination of \\( C, V, \\) and \\( S \\):\n\n1. \\( C = 3, V = 2, S = 4 \\)\n2. \\( C = 4, V = 3, S = 2 \\)\n3. \\( C = 5, V = 3, S = 1 \\)\n4. \\( C = 5, V = 2, S = 2 \\) (not valid because \\( V > S \\))\n5. \\( C = 6, V = 3, S = 0 \\) (not valid because \\( S \\geq 1 \\))\n6. \\( C = 6, V = 2, S = 1 \\) (not valid because \\( V > S \\))\n7. \\( C = 7, V = 3, S = 0 \\) (not valid because \\( S \\geq 1 \\))\n8. \\( C = 7, V = 2, S = 1 \\) (not valid because \\( V > S \\))\n9. \\( C = 8, V = 3, S = 0 \\) (not valid because \\( S \\geq 1 \\))\n10. \\( C = 8, V = 2, S = 1 \\) (not valid because \\( V > S \\))\n\nSo, the valid assignments are:\n1. \\( C = 3, V = 2, S = 4 \\)\n2. \\( C = 4, V = 3, S = 2 \\)\n3. \\( C = 5, V = 3, S = 1 \\)\n\nNow, we calculate the number of ways to assign flavors to players for each valid combination:\n1. \\( C = 3, V = 2, S = 4 \\): \\( \\binom{9}{3} \\times \\binom{6}{2} \\times \\binom{4}{4} = 84 \\times 15 \\times 1 = 1260 \\)\n2. \\( C = 4, V = 3, S = 2 \\): \\( \\binom{9}{4} \\times \\binom{5}{3} \\times \\binom{2}{2} = 126 \\times 10 \\times 1 = 1260 \\)\n3. \\( C = 5, V = 3, S = 1 \\): \\( \\binom{9}{5} \\times \\binom{4}{3} \\times \\binom{1}{1} = 126 \\times 4 \\times 1 = 504 \\)\n\nAdding these together gives the total number of assignments:\n\\[ N = 1260 + 1260 + 504 = 3024 \\]\n\nFinally, we find the remainder when \\( N \\) is divided by 1000:\n\\[ 3024 \\mod 1000 = 24 \\]\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( N \\) is divided by 1000 is \\(\\boxed{24}\\).",
